/// Streams changes from a MongoDB replica set.
///
/// Read from a MongoDB replica set using [change
/// streams](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/changeStreams/). It's only possible to
/// watch for changes when using a sharded MongoDB or a MongoDB cluster running as a
/// replica set. Use <<collections,`collections`>> to choose which collections in the
/// database to stream changes from.
///
/// If a stored resume position can no longer be resumed (for example it has aged out of
/// the oplog), the input clears its checkpoint and re-runs the snapshot rather than
/// retrying a dead position. After 2 such recoveries in a row without the change stream
/// ever advancing in between - which usually means the snapshot is taking longer than the
/// oplog window - the next failure keeps the checkpoint and the input fails on every
/// reconnect with an actionable error instead of re-snapshotting forever; after
/// addressing the cause, restart the pipeline (or delete the checkpoint cache entry) to
/// resume recovery.
///
/// With neither `stream_snapshot` nor `incremental_snapshot.enabled` set, there is no
/// snapshot to re-run, so recovery would have to skip the changes between the lost
/// position and now. The input refuses to do that silently: by default it fails with an
/// error on every reconnect and preserves the checkpoint, and skipping the gap has to be
/// opted into with `on_unresumable_position: reset`.
///
/// By default MongoDB does not propagate changes in all cases. In order to capture all
/// changes (including deletes) in a MongoDB cluster one needs to enable pre and post
/// image saving and the <<collections,collections>> also need to enable saving these pre
/// and post images. For more information see [MongoDB
/// documentation](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/changeStreams/#change-streams-with-document-pre--and-post-images)
/// and the <<document_mode,`document_mode`>> options.
///
/// ## Snapshot and streaming modes
///
/// When `stream_snapshot` is `true` and `checkpoint_cache` holds no stream position, this
/// input records the current change stream position, reads all documents in the selected
/// collections using `snapshot_parallelism` connections, and then streams the changes
/// made since the recorded position. Snapshot progress is not checkpointed, so if the
/// pipeline restarts before the snapshot completes, the snapshot runs again from the
/// start. Once the snapshot completes and is acknowledged, its position is stored in
/// `checkpoint_cache`, so later restarts resume the stream without another snapshot.
///
/// When `snapshot_parallelism` is greater than `1`, this input reads the snapshot in
/// parallel by splitting each collection into chunks with the `splitVector` command. In
/// environments where `splitVector` is not available, such as MongoDB Atlas, set
/// <<snapshot_auto_bucket_sharding,`snapshot_auto_bucket_sharding`>> to `true` to compute
/// the chunks with the `$bucketAuto` aggregation stage instead.
///
/// When `stream_snapshot` is `false` and no position is stored, this input starts
/// streaming from the latest position in the [operations log
/// (oplog)](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/replica-set-oplog/). In both modes,
/// a restart resumes streaming from the position stored in `checkpoint_cache`.
///
/// [[incremental-snapshots]]
/// ## Incremental snapshots
///
/// With `incremental_snapshot.enabled: true` the snapshot no longer holds back streaming.
/// This is mutually exclusive with `stream_snapshot`: both read the same documents, so
/// enabling both would deliver everything twice. On a first start the input streams
/// changes immediately and reads every collection in `incremental_snapshot.chunk_size`
/// chunks, in `_id` order, between them. Each chunk is released once the change stream
/// has caught up with the moment it was read, and any document the stream changed
/// meanwhile is dropped from the chunk, so a document is never emitted older than a
/// change already streamed for it. Chunks are read from the primary whatever the read
/// preference in `url`, which still applies to the change stream: a lagging secondary
/// could return documents older than changes already streamed. Progress is stored in
/// `checkpoint_cache` under `incremental_snapshot.checkpoint_cache_key`, so a restart,
/// for example after a credential expiry, continues from the last acknowledged chunk
/// rather than starting over. The progress is written again with every stream position,
/// so a cache with a TTL expires both together.
///
/// A collection added to `collections` is read on the next start, while the others carry
/// on streaming. Turning on `incremental_snapshot.enabled` in a pipeline that already has
/// a stream position stored, whether it ran with `stream_snapshot` or without it, reads
/// nothing: with a stored position and no stored snapshot progress, every collection
/// counts as read. To backfill such a pipeline, set `signal_collection_name` and send a
/// `snapshot-execute` signal. A `snapshot-execute` <<signals,signal>> reads a collection
/// on request, in every snapshot mode: the same chunked read also serves pipelines
/// running with `stream_snapshot` or with no snapshot at all.
///
/// Documents from a chunk have the operation set to "read" and the operation time of the
/// chunk read. Collections are read one at a time, so `snapshot_parallelism` and
/// `snapshot_auto_bucket_sharding` do not apply. Chunks are bounded by index ranges
/// rather than comparisons, so a collection whose `_id` values mix BSON types is read in
/// full.
///
/// A document can be delivered twice, once streamed and once in a chunk: when it is
/// inserted after streaming starts and before its chunk is read. Treat documents as
/// idempotent upserts keyed by `_id`, as is standard CDC practice. With `document_mode:
/// partial_update`, an update streamed while its document is buffered replaces the
/// chunk's full document with the update description alone, so a consumer that has not
/// seen the document before receives only the delta; prefer `update_lookup` or
/// `pre_and_post_images` with incremental snapshots. On sharded collections documents are
/// matched on `_id` alone. MongoDB does not enforce a unique `_id` across shards unless
/// `_id` is the shard key, so make sure your application keeps it unique. Otherwise the
/// snapshot can skip documents that share an `_id`.
///
/// A chunk can only be released once the change stream has passed the moment it was read.
/// On a busy deployment that happens almost at once, and on a quiet replica set the input
/// releases several chunks per poll. A quiet sharded cluster is slower: its change stream
/// only advances as far as its least active shard, which moves on with its periodic no-op
/// write, every 10 seconds by default (the shards' `periodicNoopIntervalSecs`). Expect
/// about one chunk per interval there, and raise `incremental_snapshot.chunk_size` to
/// compensate, with `checkpoint_limit` raised to at least twice that.
///
/// Progress is reported through metrics:
/// `mongodb_cdc_incremental_snapshot_collections_remaining` counts the collections still
/// to read, `mongodb_cdc_incremental_snapshot_documents_released` and
/// `mongodb_cdc_incremental_snapshot_chunks_released` count what has been released per
/// collection, and `mongodb_cdc_incremental_snapshot_chunks_deferred` counts chunk reads
/// deferred after a transient failure. The start and end of each collection's read are
/// logged at info level.
///
/// [[signals]]
/// ## Signals
///
/// When `signal_collection_name` is set, documents inserted into that collection control
/// the input while it runs. Each signal has a `type` and a `data` field, which may be an
/// embedded document or a JSON string:
///
/// - `log`: logs `data.message` at info level, for example `{"type": "log", "data":
///   {"message": "hello"}}`.
/// - `snapshot-execute`: reads the named collections incrementally, alongside streaming,
///   for example `{"type": "snapshot-execute", "data": {"collections": ["orders"]}}`
///   (`tables` is accepted as an alias of `collections`). Each named collection must also
///   be in `collections`, because the input only watches those for changes. If a signal
///   names any other collection, the input skips the whole signal and logs an error. A
///   collection already read is read again, which makes the signal a way to re-read a
///   collection on demand; one still queued or being read is not queued twice.
/// +
/// The read works the same in every snapshot mode. It does not need
/// `incremental_snapshot.enabled`: with `stream_snapshot`, or with no snapshot at all,
/// the named collections are read in `incremental_snapshot.chunk_size` chunks between
/// change events, exactly as described under <<incremental-snapshots,incremental
/// snapshots>>, and the same `retry_cooldown` and duplicate-delivery notes apply. Nothing
/// is read until a signal asks for it, a collection added to `collections` later
/// included: only `incremental_snapshot.enabled` reads one on the next start. Progress is
/// stored under `incremental_snapshot.checkpoint_cache_key` alongside the stream
/// position, so a restart continues a requested read from the last acknowledged chunk
/// rather than losing the request.
///
/// Malformed signals and unknown types are logged and skipped. Updates and deletes in the
/// signal collection are ignored.
///
/// Signal inserts are emitted as regular messages (`operation=insert`,
/// `collection=<signal_collection_name>`), the same as `postgres_cdc` and
/// `aws_dynamodb_cdc` emit their signals. To keep them out of the output, filter on the
/// `collection` metadata field:
///
/// ```yaml
/// pipeline:
///   processors:
///     - mapping: |
///         root = if @collection == "rpcn_signals" { deleted() } else { this }
/// ```
///
/// ## Scaling
///
/// All configured collections are consumed through a single database-level change stream:
/// the collection list is a server-side filter on that one stream, not a set of
/// independent streams. Because a change stream is one totally-ordered cursor over the
/// replica set's oplog, throughput does not increase with additional collections, and CPU
/// beyond roughly two cores is not used. This is a property of MongoDB change streams
/// rather than of this input. To scale beyond the single-stream ceiling, shard the
/// cluster: a change stream against a sharded cluster merges parallel per-shard cursors
/// on the server side.
///
/// Size the oplog for consumer lag: a change stream resumes from a position in the oplog,
/// so if this input falls behind, or is stopped, for longer than the oplog retains data,
/// the resume position is lost and the stream is invalidated. Ensure the oplog window
/// comfortably exceeds the longest expected lag or downtime under peak write load.
///
/// ## Metadata
///
/// Each message emitted by this plugin has the following metadata:
///
/// - `operation`: Either `insert`, `replace`, `delete`, or `update` for changes streamed.
///   Documents from a snapshot have the operation set to `read`.
/// - `collection`: The collection the document was written to.
/// - `operation_time`: The oplog time for when this operation occurred.
/// - `schema`: The collection schema in benthos common schema format (set as immutable
///   metadata). Extracted from the collection's `$jsonSchema` validator if available,
///   otherwise inferred from the first document seen. Not present on messages where no
///   schema could be determined (for example, deletes without pre-images when no prior
///   schema is cached).
///
/// ## Schema Detection
///
/// Schema metadata is discovered using a two-tier strategy:
///
/// 1. *$jsonSchema validators* are preferred and queried at startup for each watched
///    collection. When a validator exists, the schema provides accurate type information
///    and required/optional field classification.
/// 2. When no validator exists, schema is *inferred from the first document* received per
///    collection. All fields are marked optional.
///
/// *Change detection:* when a document's top-level field set differs from the cached
/// schema, the schema is re-inferred from that document. This applies to both
/// validator-sourced and inference-sourced schemas.
///
/// *Limitations:* type changes within existing fields and structural changes inside
/// nested subdocuments are not detected automatically. Restart the input to force a full
/// schema refresh.
///
/// *Fields with null values, unknown BSON types, or mixed-type arrays* are mapped to the
/// `Any` schema type. The `parquet_encode` processor does not support `Any` and will
/// error if it encounters one. Add an upstream processor (for example, `mapping`) to
/// convert or remove these fields before `parquet_encode`.
///
/// *Schema stability:* MongoDB collections may contain documents with varying field sets.
/// When this occurs, the schema updates on each structural change, which can cause
/// frequent schema version bumps in schema registries with compatibility modes. For
/// schema registry targets, configuring a `$jsonSchema` validator on the collection is
/// strongly recommended.
///
/// Status: stable
